From: Adriel Santos <adriel@adriel.eu>
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: ath11k: ahb: absorb a fatal raised during a planned root PD restart

A hw-restart of one radio stops its user PD and then restarts the root PD
(975). On an RD03v2 (NSS offload, the third of five 5 GHz hw-restarts in a
row) the user PD took 3.2 s to stop instead of 0.17 s, and the root
raised a fatal error with an empty message 1.5 ms into rproc_shutdown().
The remoteproc crash handler waited for the rproc lock, which shutdown and
then rproc_boot() held, and ran once the new root was up: it took that root
for a crashed one and restarted it again ("handling crash #1"), under the
user PD just spawned. The 5 GHz radio's restart then failed in op_start
(-108), mac80211 warned "Hardware became unavailable during restart", and
its AP was gone.

The remoteproc core already stands a crash handler down when it finds the
processor offline ("Don't recover if the remote processor was stopped").
Flush it between rproc_shutdown() and rproc_boot(), so a fatal raised by
the root being shut down is absorbed by this restart; one raised by the
new root is still recovered as usual.

Signed-off-by: Adriel Santos <adriel@adriel.eu>
---
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/ahb.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/ahb.c
@@ -550,6 +550,17 @@ static int ath11k_ahb_restart_root_pd(struct ath11k_base *ab)
 		goto out;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * The root can raise a fatal error while it is being shut down (seen
+	 * after a slow user PD stop). That is part of this restart, not a
+	 * crash to recover from as well, but its crash handler waits for the
+	 * rproc lock that rproc_shutdown() held: run after rproc_boot(), it
+	 * would find the new root running and restart it again under the user
+	 * PD being spawned. Let it run now, while the root is offline, when it
+	 * stands down.
+	 */
+	flush_work(&rproc->crash_handler);
+
 	reinit_completion(&ri->rootpd_ready);
 	ret = rproc_boot(rproc);
 	if (ret) {
